        "writtenLesson": "Act I: The Cold Open — The Death by a Thousand Revisions\n\nWinning a client at ₹15,000 is an exhilarating feeling.\nUntil two weeks into the project, when the client starts messaging you at 11:30 PM on WhatsApp:\n- \"Can you also make 5 more clips from this other video?\"\n- \"Can you change the font, re-color the background, and swap the music on all 10 videos?\"\n- \"Can you also design a YouTube thumbnail and write 3 email newsletters?\"\n\nBecause you never defined the exact boundaries of your deliverables, you feel trapped. You worry that if you push back, the client will get angry and fire you. So you comply, working 60 hours a week for a client who pays you peanuts.\n\nScope creep is not the client's fault; scope creep is the operator's failure to establish a professional Scope of Work (SOW).\n\nAct II: Itemizing the Package\n\nYour service agreement must specify deliverables like a military supply manifest:\n\nFor a ₹15,000/month Video Repurposing Retainer:\n1. Input Scope:\n   - Client provides 2 long-form recordings per month (Maximum length: 60 minutes each).\n   - Client provides high-resolution raw video and audio files via shared Google Drive.\n2. Output Scope:\n   - Exactly 12 high-retention vertical video clips per month (6 clips per recording).\n   - Duration: 30 to 60 seconds per clip.\n   - Aspect ratio: 9:16 optimized for mobile feeds.\n   - Assets include: dynamic captions, sound design, punch-in cuts, and branded color palette.\n   - Delivery cadence: 3 clips delivered every Friday.\n3. Revision Boundaries:\n   - Exactly ONE round of revisions per batch, provided within 48 hours of delivery.\n   - Revisions cover text typos, audio balance, and minor cut timing. They do not cover re-selecting completely different raw segments.\n\nAct III: What Is Explicitly Excluded\n\nA professional SOW always includes an \"Exclusions\" section:\n- \"This retainer does not include: scriptwriting, custom 3D animation, recording equipment management, social media community management, or replying to comments.\"\n\nWhen a client asks for extra work, you don't get angry; you simply say:\n\"I would love to help with that! That falls outside our core repurposing retainer, but I can easily add it as an add-on package for ₹5,000.\"\n\nAct IV: Strategic Implementation Protocols and Battle Exercises\n\nConstruct your \"Standard Scope of Work Template\":\n\nCreate a clean one-page Google Doc:\n- Header: Scope of Services & Deliverables Manifest.\n- Section 1: Client Inputs & Requirements.\n- Section 2: Concrete Weekly Deliverables.\n- Section 3: Revision Policy & Turnaround Times.\n- Section 4: Out-of-Scope Pricing Menu.\nNever begin work without the client signing or replying \"I agree to these terms\" in writing."
