        "writtenLesson": "Act I: The Cold Open — The Problem Must Be Visible\n\nHaving money is necessary, but it is not sufficient.\nA wealthy manufacturing company that produces industrial steel bearings might have ₹50 crore in revenue, but they have zero desire or need to post TikTok reels or Instagram shorts.\n\nYou need a prospect who has Money AND a Visible Problem that your service directly solves.\n\nIn Kunal Raj's content repurposing model, diagnosing the problem is astonishingly simple: you can see it with your own eyes in 60 seconds of inspection.\n\nAct II: The Anatomy of the Content Repurposing Problem\n\nWhat does a high-probability \"Problem Candidate\" look like?\nLook for these exact operational patterns:\n1. The Active Long-Form Creator:\n   - They host a weekly podcast, post 45-minute YouTube interviews, or conduct live webinar masterclasses regularly.\n   - This proves they already value content creation and have an abundant supply of raw video material.\n2. The Short-Form Ghost Town:\n   - When you check their Instagram Reels, YouTube Shorts, or LinkedIn feeds, they post irregularly: once every three weeks, or nothing at all.\n   - Or, their short-form clips look terrible: raw Zoom recordings with no captions, bad audio levels, zero hooks, and awkward pauses.\n3. The Apologetic Statement:\n   - The founder frequently says on social media: \"I wish I had more time to post on LinkedIn, but our team is swamped right now.\"\n   - They are publicly confessing their operational bottleneck!\n\nAct III: The Power of Pre-Diagnosed Pain\n\nWhen you reach out to someone who visibly has this gap, you do not need to persuade them that short-form distribution is valuable. They already know they are falling behind!\nYour message arrives as an answer to an existing internal frustration:\n\"Finally, someone noticed that we have 40 podcast episodes sitting idle and has the capability to fix it for us.\"\n\nAct IV: Strategic Implementation Protocols and Battle Exercises\n\nExecute the \"Problem Hunting Checklist\":\n\nOpen YouTube and search for:\n- \"Indian B2B SaaS podcast\"\n- \"Healthcare entrepreneur interview\"\n- \"Real estate investment podcast India\"\n- \"D2C founder story\"\n\nFind 5 channels where:\n- The host publishes long-form episodes consistently (at least 2 per month).\n- The \"Shorts\" tab on their channel has fewer than 5 videos published in the last 60 days.\nBookmark these 5 channels. These are your prime problem candidates."
