        "writtenLesson": "Act I: The Cold Open — The First Impression After Payment\n\nThe moment a client pays your ₹15,000 invoice, they experience a brief spike of buyer's remorse:\n\"Did I just waste money on a stranger on the internet? Are they going to ghost me? Is this going to be a messy headache?\"\n\nIf you disappear for 4 days without an update, or email them asking: \"Hey, what format do you want your files in again?\", their anxiety spikes. They regret hiring you before you have delivered a single file.\n\nYour onboarding process must be so organized, crisp, and professional that the client immediately thinks: \"Wow, these people run a world-class operation.\"\n\nAct II: The Standardized Drive Vault\n\nCreate an immutable folder hierarchy inside Google Drive for every client:\n- Root Folder: `[Client_Name] - Content Repurposing Hub`\n  - `01_RAW_FOOTAGE/` (Where the client drops unedited video links)\n  - `02_BRAND_ASSETS/` (Client logos, fonts, color hex codes, intro/outro stings)\n  - `03_FOR_REVIEW/` (Where you upload finished clips for their feedback)\n  - `04_APPROVED_FINAL_ASSETS/` (Where approved, polished files live)\n\nAct III: Strict File Naming Discipline\n\nNever, under any circumstances, name a file `video_final_v2_edit.mp4`. That screams amateurism.\n\nAdopt this professional naming standard:\n`[Ep#]_[Clip#]_[Topic_Hook]_[Date].mp4`\nExample: `Ep42_Clip01_Why_Hiring_Fails_Nov2026.mp4`\n\nAccompany every video upload with a clean `.txt` document:\n`Ep42_Clip01_Caption_and_Hooks.txt`\nContaining:\n- Recommended Headline\n- 3-line post copy formatted with emojis and line breaks\n- Suggested hashtags\n\nThe client or their social media coordinator can open the folder, download the video, copy the text, and publish in 15 seconds. You have eliminated all friction.\n\nAct IV: Strategic Implementation Protocols and Battle Exercises\n\nBuild your \"Master Client Onboarding Template\":\n\nCreate a master folder template in Google Drive.\nWhen a new client signs, duplicate the template, name it with their company handle, and share view/edit access in your welcome email.\nDeliver your first batch 24 hours ahead of schedule. Exceeding expectations on Batch #1 guarantees client retention."
